oint  and when i say worst case i mean  a peak that is extremely narrow and so  what you see is in the  the fastest response time which is .05  seconds you're seeing  you're getting much closer to the actual  the true  chromatographic peak there when the blue  trace  but as you increase the response time  the signal  the measured signal actually becomes is  looks like it's shifting later and also  getting broader  again that's just simply from a delay in  how quickly the instrument the detector  is responding to the signal  and um you know just to say that you  know this was done as it shows here  at a 20 hertz sampling rate that's 20  points per second that was more than  sufficient  for this example but  you know as with sampling rate um the  real downside to do  doing having a response time that's  faster than necessary  is you get excess noise so again it's  something you want to have sort of  that's optimized more or less i can tell  you for an actual  you know real chromatogram you're  there's no way you need to go faster  than 0.1 seconds in fact for  you 0.2 seconds may be quite sufficient  again that's just something you you  would maybe want to  to try and just confirm that everything  looks good that way but  um yeah  so yeah uh summary uh what we talked  about here then  is um instrument selection and column  selection how they're interrelated you  know you've got to have the right  instrument and instrument configuration  for the type column you want to use  and and that's not so much about the  particle type but a combination of  particle type and particle size  and so likewise you know it goes the  other way if you have you know you know  you want to use a  the best column you can for your  instrument you also want to make sure  then of course that you have  the appropriate instrument for that  so yeah we talked about these column  technologies the choices and  implications the comparison of  performance between the two different  types  and then you know instrument  configuration you know focused on  minimizing system volume  to minimizing tubing length um  tubing diameter is critical  you have to you know take into account  the pressure considerations as well so  again i recommend  you know a 100 to 125 micron id for that  works very well for uh these fuse core  columns  and then also the flow cell um you want  to make sure the flow cell volume is not  more than five microliters  and we just uh discussed data collection  as well with sampling rates need a fast  enough sampling rate to make sure you  have at least 10 points across the  narrowest peak  and the detector response time needs to  be fast enough as well but again going  too fast uh only  causes more noise than and that's not  anything desirable of course  so with that i thank you very much  today my name is trisha schnelly and i'm  going to be taking the next  minutes to walk you through some hints  and tips for your cannabis analyses for  your high purity water  ultra pure water is highly prone to  contamination  and as the most frequently used solvent  in lcms  laboratories its purity plays a critical  role  in your analyses and so what we're going  to go over today are just a few  quick things you can do in the lab very  easily regarding your water storage  your glassware cleaning your consumables  and last but certainly not least  the usage of your water purification  system  so but before we dive into those tips  and tricks i do want to take a moment to  discuss  why contaminated why having contaminated  high purity water can be problematic  in your laboratory so first is  difficulties  in interpreting your data if you if your  water is contaminated you'll end up with  background noise in your chromatograms  it also can decrease the lifetime of  your columns  and increase the maintenance of your  instruments so we want to make sure we  avoid that but  to me most importantly it's the risk of  repeated experiments  which leads to waste of sample reagents  solvents  and time and we know that all of that  equals money  so to save money and to save time and  save your results  we want to make sure you're getting the  most out of your high purity water that  you can  so the first thing we're going to talk  about is your water storage  and so you may not think about water  storage as being an  issue but there's actually two places  where you can  you can see increased where you can  experience problems and that's actually  contamination from the environment  as well as contamination from the  container  these are two areas we're going to  discuss over the next couple slides and  demonstrate to you  why why these things need to be taken  into consideration  the first is avoiding repeated opening  of stored high purity water so when  you're storing high purity water over a  length of time and you're  you're opening the bottle and closing  the bottle and opening and closing  over a period of time you're exposing it  to those environmental contaminants  and so in this particular experiment we  took bottled ultrapure water and we  stored it for four weeks  and we used it we we opened it and  closed it like you would in any normal  laboratory situation and to replicate  that  and then here on the bottom we just use  freshly produced millicu  ultra pure water through our through the  lcms  and so you can see here from this this  top chromatogram which is the bottled  water you can see you have some peaks  and some background noise that you  certainly wouldn't want to have you can  see down here  um by using fresh water that you  actually  get rid of that background noise for  your experiments  so that's that's storing your water and  why  how the environment impacts it but you  may be asking well  how does the container i actually store  it in cause problems well  some people don't think about this but  plastic can actually be problematic for  storage of your high purity  water we recommend using brown glass  bottles  for your storage but people commonly use  plastic  carboys and things to store their their  water so we've  we've taken water and stored it in a  brown glass bottle and a polypropylene  bottle  and then took the water out of it and  did lcms to see  what kind of results we had and so here  we on the left we have  the brown glass bottle you can see no  peaks no background noise  you come over to the polypropylene  bottle you  get anti-static agent peaks you get  peaks from stabilizers  and then down here on the tic intensity  you can see  low level background from the brown  bottle but the higher level from the  polypropylene bottle so you can see that  that prop  polypropylene can leach  agents into your water that can cause  background  and so ultimately we just want to  remember  in when it comes to water storage just  use freshly purified water whenever  possible it will make your life easier  and take out some of that questions  about where some of those peaks may be  coming from if it's not what you expect  to see  so moving on we're next going to talk  about glassware cleaning  and the take home here is to avoid  dishwashers um  is our general rule of thumb within  within millipore sigma  and so we we actually in this study we  looked at  containers washed in a dishwasher versus  containers washed with acetonitrile  and ultra pure water and so you can see  we ran it through  our analysis and you can see here in the  washed and dishwasher you can see all  these  background peaks compared to containers  washed with acetonitrile and ultra pure  water  and we even identified some of these as  triton detergents  within these peaks so although  dishwashers  are incredibly convenient they do leave  residue  behind that can contaminate your water  so thus it's important  to wash your glassware correctly so just  always remember your container should be  carefully rinsed  to ensure that there is no detergent  residue that  contaminates your water  moving on we're then going to talk about  your consumable usage or your  your cartridges and filters and of  course we want you to replace those  um regularly and this is this is for  your your benefit to do this um because  it can  it can really impact your your  background noise and the purity of your  water  we also we also recommend you rinse your  new cartridges  following the user manual instructions  in case anyone's interested  our new um millicu iq  and ix 7000 series showed here  they have consumables that can be pla  replaced with one hand  so no more having the sign next to the  system  telling you how to change the consumable  because it's complicated  and in addition the user manual is  actually stored here on a touch screen  touch screen so you never have to go  digging through the drawer  drawer wondering where the manual is but  talking about these consumables and why  they need to be changed  um we we're going to look at  specifically final filters  at this point and so this is what a  final filter looks like  it is what is at the end of the the  point of use  um so it is like it's called it's a  final filter  um and in this situation what we what we  studied is we studied fresh ultrapure  water from a millicue system with a  filter that was relatively new  and then or within the time range we  typically  expect people to use these final filters  we actually  recommend they get changed every three  to four months and then  compared it to fresh ultra pure water  from a millicu system with an  old final filter and specifically 18  months old  and you may think that's crazy  considering we we recommend  every three to four months however 18  months is  not a crazy number we've actually seen  this  very regular regularly in laboratories  that we visited  and so this isn't a completely obscene  number  um and so we ran we ran this water  through a uh plc  system and you can see the chromatogram  here on the left  and you can see that with the ultra pure  water with the old final filter you get  this you get this random peak  and so obviously that can be problematic  to the work that you're  you're doing and so the take-home point  here  is change your cartridges and final  filters as recommended  we recommend those timelines for a  reason and the reason  is the that chromatogram on the that you  just saw  that can happen to you  and then last but certainly not least we  want to talk about  using your water purification system and  specifically about  when you come back on a monday from the  system not being used for a couple days  or if  if work has been down due to the  pandemic and you come back  after weeks of not using it this is even  more critical at this point in time but  what what we do here is we we really  recommend flushing  the water so in this particular study we  took  milliculture pure water on monday after  it was stood in standby  over the weekend and we ran an lcms on  it  we also took water from a millicue  system on monday  but discarded the first four leaders out  of the system  to do that flush and then have that  chromatograph down here and so when you  compare these chromatographs  you can see that using the water that's  in the system and not flushing it first  can cause some background noise that you  certainly don't want  whereas if it's fresh water after  discarding  you get no background and that's exactly  what you want to see  so as a reminder don't forget to flush  it's it's incredibly important to do  because you don't want to get any  contaminants  in your in your analyses  so i just want to take a moment and  review what we went over  i know it's not a lot but it's it's very  impactful and it's  been in a short period of time so make  sure you use fresh water whenever  possible  avoid dishwashers and carefully rinse  your glassware  change your cartridges as recommended  and flush your system  after periods of lack of use and so  that concludes this workshop today i  hope you  learned a little something maybe can  apply to your lab  and thank you so much for your time and  enjoy the rest of your day
 

